n{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{1}=(0)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The fact that we got the zero-vector as the projection means that $x$ is orthogonal to the subspace $S.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-40615/selection